\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Workflow of the study: from the measured curves and the literature to the V1 ATLAS model, its calibration, the out-of-sample and post hoc tests, the mechanism verdicts, the registered predictions and the validation status. Evidence classes as in \cref {ch:nomenclature}.}}{3}{figure.caption.14}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Cross-section of the modelled device (not to scale): TiN gate \SI {50}{nm}, HfO$_2${} \SI {15}{nm}, Al$_2$O$_3${} \SI {2}{nm}, IWO channel of thickness $t$, Pd source/drain \SI {70}{nm} overlapping the channel by \SI {2}{\micro \metre } on each side, air above the channel; $L = \SI {20}{\micro \metre }$, $W = \SI {290}{\micro \metre }$. The ATLAS region numbers of the physical-structure deck are indicated (1: IWO bulk; 2: \SI {0.25}{nm} IWO interface layer; 3: Al$_2$O$_3${}; 4: HfO$_2${}; 6: Conductor gate volume; 7: Air). Drawn from \file {config/geometry.yaml} and \file {docs/DEVICE_STRUCTURE.md}.}}{29}{figure.caption.60}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Measured transfer curves of the 2.0, 6.3, 13.2 and \SI {31.8}{nm} devices at $V_{\mathrm {D}}= \SI {0.7}{V}$ (\file {data/experimental_clean.csv}). (a) Logarithmic scale; the dotted lines mark the off-band medians ($-2 \le V_{\mathrm {G}}\le -0.5$\,V): $4.61\times 10^{-15}$ (2.0 nm), $1.53\times 10^{-13}$ (6.3 nm) and $6.60\times 10^{-12}\ensuremath {\,\mathrm {A}/\mu \mathrm {m}}$ (13.2 nm). (b) Linear scale. The 2.0 and \SI {6.3}{nm} curves are close to each other, the \SI {13.2}{nm} curve is shifted to lower gate voltage and carries about six times more on-current, and the \SI {31.8}{nm} device does not turn off within the sweep.}}{31}{figure.caption.65}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Measured metrics versus channel thickness (2.0, 6.3, \SI {13.2}{nm}; \SI {31.8}{nm} as a hollow symbol where defined): $V_{\mathrm {th,cc}}$ and $V_{\mathrm {th,lin}}$, fixed-current swings $\mathit {SS}_{[10^{-11},\,10^{-10}]}$ and $\mathit {SS}_{[10^{-10},\,10^{-8}]}$, linear $\mu _{\mathrm {FE}}$ and saturation-formula mobility, $I_{\mathrm {on}}$ and the off-floor. Same numbers as \cref {tab:measured-metrics}. The step between 6.3 and \SI {13.2}{nm} in threshold, mobility and on-current, and the relation $\mu _{\mathrm {FE}}(6.3) < \mu _{\mathrm {FE}}(2.0)$, are visible.}}{33}{figure.caption.67}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Thickness laws of the V1 model. (a) $\Delta E_{\mathrm {c}}(t)$: the ATLAS law $0.9205\,t^{-1.3815}$~eV (\cref {eq:dec-law}) against the infinite-well effective-mass $E_1$ for $m^{*}= 0.18$, 0.26 and 0.35~$m_0$, the PBE slab points of \citet [p.~21542]{Lin2022} and \citet [p.~504]{Si2021}, and the Schr\"odinger--Poisson subthreshold-equivalent shifts of S3 (case Q1 at $n_s = 10^{10}\ensuremath {\,\mathrm {cm}^{-2}}$). Above 3~nm the power law diverges from the $t^{-2}$ limit. (b) $m^{*}(t)$ law with the PBE slab points. (c) $N_{\mathrm {t}}(t)$ power law against the surface\,+\,bulk alternative through the same anchors. (d) $N_{\mathrm {d,eff}}(t)$. (e) Roughness factor $(1-\Delta _{\mathrm {sr}}/t)^2$ (used in \cref {sec:par-rough}). Laws from \file {config/iwo_material_model.yaml}; DFT points from \file {tables/THICKNESS_LAW_EVIDENCE.csv}; SP shifts from \file {scratch/S3/s3_sp1d_notraps_out.txt}.}}{41}{figure.caption.105}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces S3 1-D Schr\"odinger--Poisson surrogate (trap-free, \file {scratch/S3/s3_sp1d_notraps.json}): electron sheet density $n_s(V_{\mathrm {G}})$, quantum vs classical, at 2~nm and 6.3~nm, with the onset shift and the electron centroid. Shifts vs the classical case C0 at $10^{10}\ensuremath {\,\mathrm {cm}^{-2}}$: C1 (ATLAS law) 0.345 / 0.070 / 0.026~V and Q1 0.256 / 0.036 / 0.016~V at 2 / 6.3 / 13.2~nm. The shift is rigid at 2~nm, and at 6.3~nm the law exceeds the quantum shift by about a factor 2.}}{43}{figure.caption.114}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Energy-band diagrams of the calibrated decks \texttt {run\_0012} (2.0~nm, panels a--c) and \texttt {run\_0013} (13.2~nm, panel d), as exported by the package plotting scripts (\file {plots/2p0/band_diagram_{eq,vth,on}.png}, \file {plots/13p2/band_diagram_on.png}). The panels show the conduction-band edge of the 2~nm film raised by confinement; the almost flat bands inside the film at threshold (ATLAS band bending across the film 0.022~eV at 2~nm and 0.016~eV at 13.2~nm, S1), which justifies the additive budget of \cref {tab:d1-budget}; and the front-interface band bending in the on-state.}}{57}{figure.caption.198}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Trap-DOS spectroscopy by the charge-sheet inversion (\cref {eq:d1-ns-sum,eq:d1-dtrap}) of S4 (copied from \file {scratch/S4/s4_trap_spectroscopy.png}): trapped-state density vs $u = E_{Fn}-E_{\mathrm {c}}$ from the measured curves and from the simulated curves of the calibrated decks. The figure shows the agreement of measured and ATLAS DOS within 0.81--1.17 for $u = -0.125$ to $-0.025$~eV (2 and 13.2~nm); the sub-linear growth of the near-edge sheet DOS with thickness; the underestimate of the deeper states by V1.}}{59}{figure.caption.203}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Electron density vs gate voltage in the calibrated decks, as exported by the package plotting scripts (\file {plots/2p0/electron_density_vs_vg.png}, \file {plots/13p2/electron_density_vs_vg.png}). The thin film fills uniformly (volumetric conduction, centroid $\approx t/2$), whereas the 13.2~nm film develops a front accumulation layer of about 1~nm at high $V_{\mathrm {G}}$ (S1).}}{60}{figure.caption.209}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Mobility extraction from the measured transfer curves at $V_{\mathrm {D}}=\SI {0.7}{V}$ for 2.0, 6.3 and 13.2 nm. The linear-regime estimator $\mu _{\mathrm {FE}}(V_{\mathrm {G}})=Lg_{\mathrm {m}}/(WC_{\mathrm {ox}}V_{\mathrm {D}})$ (\cref {eq:mufe-lin}) is compared with the saturation formula $(2L/WC_{\mathrm {ox}})(\partial \sqrt {I_{\mathrm {D}}}/\partial V_{\mathrm {G}})^{2}$ (\cref {eq:mu-sat}). Derivatives use \texttt {numpy.gradient} on the measured 0.05\,V grid. The peaks are 12.15 / 10.95 / 50.17\,$\ensuremath {\,\mathrm {cm}^{2}/\mathrm {V\,s}}$ (linear) against 5.09 / 3.86 / 27.40\,$\ensuremath {\,\mathrm {cm}^{2}/\mathrm {V\,s}}$ (saturation formula). The target paper quotes 5.1 and 27.4\,$\ensuremath {\,\mathrm {cm}^{2}/\mathrm {V\,s}}$ for 2 and 13.2 nm. Note that the 2 nm linear estimator is still rising at the end of the sweep.}}{71}{figure.caption.246}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Y-function and H-function analysis of the measured transfer curves at $V_{\mathrm {D}}=\SI {0.7}{V}$ (existing analysis figure, \file {analysis_2026-09-25/scratch/S5/s5_yfunction_hfunction.png}). $Y=I_{\mathrm {D}}/\sqrt {g_{\mathrm {m}}}$ gives $\mu _0$ from its slope, and the H-function slope $1/(\alpha +1)$ gives the exponent without a threshold. All films show $\theta _{\mathrm {net}}<0$ and $\alpha _H>1$ (1.59 / 2.14 / 1.29), the tail-filling signature rather than the series-resistance signature. The 31.8 nm film has two $g_{\mathrm {m}}$ maxima, so its Y-function is invalid.}}{75}{figure.caption.263}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Leave-one-out tests of thickness laws for the measured $\mu _{\mathrm {FE}}(t)$ and $I_{\mathrm {on}}(t)$. Power law, $A+B/t$, exponential and step forms are fitted through 2 and 13.2 nm and used to predict 6.3 nm (measured $\mu _{\mathrm {FE}}=10.95\,\ensuremath {\,\mathrm {cm}^{2}/\mathrm {V\,s}}$; predictions 28.78, 42.73, 20.94 and 12.15). The panels also show the extrapolation to 31.8 nm. An $I_{\mathrm {on}}$ power law through 6.3 and 13.2 nm (exponent 2.75) predicts $3.43\times 10^{-5}\ensuremath {\,\mathrm {A}/\mu \mathrm {m}}$ against $3.59\times 10^{-6}$ measured. Note the non-monotonic data: no monotonic law passes through all three films.}}{84}{figure.caption.303}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Numerical convergence of the final decks. (a) DOS discretization at \SI {2}{nm}: $\Delta \log _{10}I_{\mathrm {D}}$ versus $V_{\mathrm {G}}$ for 192/96 (\texttt {run\_0019}) and 384/192 (\texttt {run\_0029}) relative to 96/48 (\texttt {run\_0012}). (b) Mesh refinement by 0.7 at \SI {13.2}{nm} (\texttt {run\_0028} vs \texttt {run\_0013}) and at \SI {6.3}{nm} (\texttt {run\_0036} vs \texttt {run\_0015}). (c) $I_{\mathrm {on}}$ at 384/192 relative to 96/48 versus thickness ($+2.5$, $+1.0$, $+0.5$\,\%; S6 report, B1--B3). (d) The $\mathit {SS}_{\min }$ artefact: local swing versus $\log _{10}I_{\mathrm {D}}$ for \texttt {run\_0012} and \texttt {run\_0038}, with the five-times-floor window marked; the minimum occurs at currents near the artefact threshold $2.30\times 10^{-14}\ensuremath {\,\mathrm {A}/\mu \mathrm {m}}$ (S6), where the two runs differ although their curves are otherwise identical.}}{98}{figure.caption.320}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Execution chain of a V1 launch. A campaign plan lists dotted-path overrides of the configuration; each entry is turned into a deck by the generator, launched once in the real solver, validated and scored. No step replaces the solver by a surrogate.}}{99}{figure.caption.322}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces Staged calibration flow. Each stage adjusts one class of quantity whose action on the transfer curve is known exactly: a rigid shift for $Q_{\mathrm {f}}$ (stage 2) and a proportional current scaling for $\mu _{\mathrm {band}}$ (stages 3 and 6). Stage 4 is the only out-of-sample test and failed for the threshold (\cref {ch:6p3}); the 6.3~nm device was then given its own $Q_{\mathrm {f}}$ and $\mu _{\mathrm {band}}$ (device-specific tuning, \texttt {run\_0007}, \texttt {run\_0015}, \texttt {run\_0039}).}}{103}{figure.caption.327}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.2}{\ignorespaces Staged calibration of the two anchor films, $\log _{10}I_{\mathrm {D}}$ versus $V_{\mathrm {G}}$ at $V_{\mathrm {D}}=\SI {0.7}{V}$ against the measured curves. 2.0~nm: \texttt {run\_0001} (baseline laws, $Q_{\mathrm {f}}=0$) $\rightarrow $ \texttt {run\_0003} (shared $Q_{\mathrm {f}}=1.73\times 10^{12}\ensuremath {\,\mathrm {cm}^{-2}}$) $\rightarrow $ \texttt {run\_0008} ($\mu _{\mathrm {band}}=18.13$) $\rightarrow $ \texttt {run\_0012} (physical deck, stepped sweep) $\rightarrow $ \texttt {run\_0038} (DOS 384/192, $\mu _{\mathrm {band}}=17.69$). 13.2~nm: \texttt {run\_0002} $\rightarrow $ \texttt {run\_0004} $\rightarrow $ \texttt {run\_0009} $\rightarrow $ \texttt {run\_0013} $\rightarrow $ \texttt {run\_0040}. The $Q_{\mathrm {f}}$ step is a pure horizontal translation, the mobility step a pure vertical scaling of the above-threshold current, and the last two steps are not visible at this scale. Metrics are in \cref {tab:cal-history}.}}{104}{figure.caption.329}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.3}{\ignorespaces Final converged calibration against the measured transfer curves ($V_{\mathrm {D}}=\SI {0.7}{V}$): \texttt {run\_0038} (2.0~nm, $\times 0.999983$), \texttt {run\_0039} $\times 1.015074$ (6.3~nm) and \texttt {run\_0040} $\times 1.020113$ (13.2~nm). Row 1: $\log _{10}I_{\mathrm {D}}$; row 2: linear $I_{\mathrm {D}}$; row 3: log residual. Simulated metrics: $V_{\mathrm {th,cc}}$ 0.680 / 0.653 / 0.054~V, $V_{\mathrm {th,lin}}$ 1.537 / 1.466 / 0.995~V, $\mu _{\mathrm {FE}}$ 11.10 / 8.39 / 48.85~\ensuremath {\,\mathrm {cm}^{2}/\mathrm {V\,s}}, $I_{\mathrm {on}}$ $5.09\times 10^{-7}$ / $4.034\times 10^{-7}$ / $3.071\times 10^{-6}\ensuremath {\,\mathrm {A}/\mu \mathrm {m}}$; active RMSE (measured current above five times the off-band median) 0.0429 / 0.0630 / 0.0813~dec. The late, steep 6.3~nm turn-on is not reproduced by the model (\cref {sec:6p3-shape}), and a smaller on-state residual of the same sign appears at 2~nm.}}{107}{figure.caption.331}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.4}{\ignorespaces Effect of each parameter on the 2.0~nm transfer curve ($\log _{10}I_{\mathrm {D}}$); each panel shows a perturbation against its baseline and the measured curve (grey circles). $Q_{\mathrm {f}}$ $0\rightarrow 1.73\times 10^{12}\ensuremath {\,\mathrm {cm}^{-2}}$ (\texttt {run\_0001}$\rightarrow $\texttt {run\_0003}); $\mu _{\mathrm {band}}$ $16.8\rightarrow 18.13$ (\texttt {run\_0003}$\rightarrow $\texttt {run\_0008}); $\chi $ $-0.1$ / $+0.1$~eV (\texttt {run\_0020} / \texttt {run\_0021} vs \texttt {run\_0012}); $N_{\mathrm {d}}$ $\times 2$ (\texttt {run\_0022}); $N_{\mathrm {t}}$ $\times 1.5$ (\texttt {run\_0023}); $W_{\mathrm {TA}}$ $+5$~meV (\texttt {run\_0024}); $D_{\mathrm {it}}$ $\times 3$ (\texttt {run\_0025}); $\varepsilon _{\mathrm {IWO}}=10.55$ (\texttt {run\_0026}); confinement off (\texttt {run\_0016}); gate WF $+0.1$~eV (\texttt {run\_0048} vs \texttt {run\_0038}); $m^{*}$ $\times 1.3$ (\texttt {run\_0050} vs \texttt {run\_0038}). \texttt {run\_0027} (overlap) is excluded as malformed. Four very different physical quantities ($Q_{\mathrm {f}}$, $\chi $, WF and, to a good approximation, confinement) produce the same horizontal translation, whereas only $N_{\mathrm {t}}$ changes the turn-on delay.}}{110}{figure.caption.337}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.5}{\ignorespaces (a) $\Delta \log _{10}I_{\mathrm {D}}(V_{\mathrm {G}})$ = perturbed $-$ baseline for every one-at-a-time case at 2.0~nm. (b) Bars of $\Delta V_{\mathrm {th,cc}}$ (V), $\Delta \mathit {SS}_{[10^{-10},\,10^{-9}]}$ (mV/dec) and $\Delta I_{\mathrm {on}}$ (\%): $Q_{\mathrm {f}}$ $0\rightarrow 1.73\times 10^{12}$: $-0.309$, $+0.4$, $+27.1$; $\mu _{\mathrm {band}}$ $16.8\rightarrow 18.13$: $-0.009$, $-3.8$, $+7.9$; $\chi $ $-0.1$~eV: $+0.100$, $0.0$, $-6.9$; $\chi $ $+0.1$~eV: $-0.100$, $0.0$, $+7.0$; $N_{\mathrm {d}}$ $\times 2$: $-0.009$, $+0.7$, $+0.7$; $N_{\mathrm {t}}$ $\times 1.5$: $+0.082$, $+49.0$, $-25.1$; $W_{\mathrm {TA}}$ $+5$~meV: $+0.020$, $+4.6$, $+0.4$; $D_{\mathrm {it}}$ $\times 3$: $+0.025$, $+0.1$, $-1.8$; $\varepsilon _{\mathrm {IWO}}$ 10.55: $-0.001$, $-0.4$, $+0.7$; confinement off: $-0.316$, $+15.3$, $+21.4$; gate WF $+0.1$~eV: $+0.100$, $0.0$, $-6.8$; $m^{*}$ $\times 1.3$: $-0.044$, $-18.0$, $+4.1$. Rigid parameters give flat difference curves in the subthreshold region and a decaying difference above threshold (because $I_{\mathrm {on}}$ is read at fixed $V_{\mathrm {G}}$).}}{111}{figure.caption.338}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.6}{\ignorespaces Parameter control at 13.2~nm: $Q_{\mathrm {f}}$ $0\rightarrow 1.73\times 10^{12}\ensuremath {\,\mathrm {cm}^{-2}}$ (\texttt {run\_0002}$\rightarrow $\texttt {run\_0004}), $\mu _{\mathrm {band}}$ $57.6\rightarrow 61.9$ (\texttt {run\_0004}$\rightarrow $\texttt {run\_0009}), confinement off (\texttt {run\_0017} vs \texttt {run\_0013}), gate WF $+0.1$~eV (\texttt {run\_0049} vs \texttt {run\_0040}) and $m^{*}$ $\times 1.3$ (\texttt {run\_0051} vs \texttt {run\_0040}), against the measured 13.2~nm curve. Switching confinement off changes the 13.2~nm threshold by only $-23$~mV, against $-316$~mV at 2~nm.}}{111}{figure.caption.339}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.1}{\ignorespaces The 6.3~nm device against every campaign-A variant (DOS 96/48): measured curve versus \texttt {run\_0014} (shared-parameter test), \texttt {run\_0015} (tuned $Q_{\mathrm {f}}$ and $\mu _{\mathrm {band}}$), A1 \texttt {run\_0031} ($t=5.3$~nm), A2 \texttt {run\_0032} (donors removed), A3 \texttt {run\_0030} (back charge $-1.64\times 10^{12}\ensuremath {\,\mathrm {cm}^{-2}}$), A4 \texttt {run\_0033} ($D_{\mathrm {it}}\times 5$), A5 \texttt {run\_0035} (combination) and A6 \texttt {run\_0034} (no confinement): $\log _{10}I_{\mathrm {D}}$ and a linear on-state panel. Every variant that moves the threshold to the data leaves the linear on-state below and to the left of the measurement.}}{118}{figure.caption.345}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.2}{\ignorespaces Errors of every 6.3~nm hypothesis relative to the measurement ($V_{\mathrm {th,cc}}$ 0.644~V, $\mathit {SS}_{\mathrm {cc}}$ 295.4~mV/dec, gap 1.176~V, $g_{\mathrm {m,max}}$ $3.431\times 10^{-7}$~A/V/\textmu m); green marks agreement within 0.05~V / 20~mV/dec / 0.1~V / 8~\%. $V_{\mathrm {th,cc}}$, $\mathit {SS}_{\mathrm {cc}}$, gap and $g_{\mathrm {m,max}}$ errors: \texttt {run\_0014} $-0.294$~V, $-10.4$, $-0.314$~V, $-16.6$~\%; \texttt {run\_0015} $+0.007$~V, $-6.5$, $-0.356$~V, $-23.1$~\%; A1 \texttt {run\_0031} $-0.258$~V, $-4.9$, $-0.297$~V, $-18.1$~\%; A2 \texttt {run\_0032} $-0.264$~V, $-14.7$, $-0.329$~V, $-17.5$~\%; A3 \texttt {run\_0030} $-0.017$~V, $-64.1$, $-0.452$~V, $-18.4$~\%; A4 \texttt {run\_0033} $-0.243$~V, $-9.1$, $-0.319$~V, $-16.9$~\%; A5 \texttt {run\_0035} $-0.079$~V, $-45.9$, $-0.405$~V, $-19.6$~\%; A6 \texttt {run\_0034} $-0.057$~V, $-7.7$, $-0.337$~V, $-18.4$~\%; B0 \texttt {run\_0037} $+0.010$~V, $+115.6$, $+0.026$~V, $-5.0$~\%; C1 \texttt {run\_0052} $+0.004$~V, $+9.1$, $-0.237$~V, $-3.5$~\%; B2 \texttt {run\_0039} $+0.009$~V, $-5.7$, $-0.362$~V, $-23.3$~\%. No case is green in all four metrics.}}{119}{figure.caption.346}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.3}{\ignorespaces The 6.3~nm on-state shape: measured curve against B2 (\texttt {run\_0039}, rescaled by 11.581987/11.41), B0 (\texttt {run\_0037}, tail re-partition) and C1 (\texttt {run\_0052}, near-$E_{\mathrm {c}}$ acceptor band). (a) $\log _{10}I_{\mathrm {D}}$; (b) linear $I_{\mathrm {D}}$; (c) $g_{\mathrm {m}}(V_{\mathrm {G}})$ from the numerical gradient on the 0.05~V grid. B0 reproduces the on-state (b, c) but not the subthreshold region (a); C1 retains the subthreshold behaviour and recovers only part of the delayed, steep rise; B2 rises too early and too slowly.}}{121}{figure.caption.355}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.1}{\ignorespaces Threshold charge budget per film. Grouped bars give the Gauss-law terms at $V_{\mathrm {th,cc}}$ from the S1 1-D surrogate ($\Delta E_{\mathrm {c}}$, filled tail, $qN_{\mathrm {d}}t/C_{\mathrm {ox}}$, interface traps, $Q_{\mathrm {f}}$, $E_{Fn}-E_{\mathrm {c}}$), which agrees with ATLAS within \SI {3}{mV} for runs \texttt {run\_0012} (2 nm), \texttt {run\_0014} (6.3 nm, shared parameters) and \texttt {run\_0013} (13.2 nm); markers give the measured $V_{\mathrm {th,cc}}$. The \SI {0.281}{V} fall of $\Delta E_{\mathrm {c}}$ from 2 to \SI {6.3}{nm} is not compensated by any other term in the shared-parameter model, which is why \texttt {run\_0014} misses the measured \SI {6.3}{nm} threshold by $-\SI {0.294}{V}$.}}{127}{figure.caption.362}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.2}{\ignorespaces Partition of the apparent mobility, $\mu _{\mathrm {FE}}= \mu _{\mathrm {band}}\times R(t) \times P$ (\cref {eq:mech-mu-partition}), per film. Left: the V1 calibration (runs \texttt {run\_0012}, \texttt {run\_0015}, \texttt {run\_0013}); right: the S2 shape-consistent partition from the 1-D surrogate, $\mu _{\mathrm {band}}$ = 19.3/19.6/$62.7\ensuremath {\,\mathrm {cm}^{2}/\mathrm {V\,s}}$ with $N_{\mathrm {t}}$ = 2.4e19/2.7e19/4.8e18 $\mathrm {cm^{-3}}$. In both partitions the roughness factor and the partition factor change by only a few per cent between 6.3 and \SI {13.2}{nm}, so the $\times 4.6$ step lies in $\mu _{\mathrm {band}}$.}}{129}{figure.caption.367}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.3}{\ignorespaces Symmetric calibration of the thresholds with and without the confinement law. (a) Front charge $Q_{\mathrm {f}}$ required by each film to reproduce its measured $V_{\mathrm {th,cc}}$: with confinement 1.81/0.09/1.56 and without 0.048/$-0.27$/1.43 (units of $10^{12}\ensuremath {\,\mathrm {cm}^{-2}}$; 2/6.3/13.2\,nm). (b) Residuals of the best single shared $Q_{\mathrm {f}}$: with confinement $Q_{\mathrm {f}}= 1.153\times 10^{12}\ensuremath {\,\mathrm {cm}^{-2}}$, residuals $+0.117/-0.19/+0.073$\,V (rms \SI {0.1358}{V}); without, $0.403\times 10^{12}\ensuremath {\,\mathrm {cm}^{-2}}$, residuals $-0.063/-0.12/+0.184$\,V (rms \SI {0.1320}{V}) (figure data from S1 key numbers / SYNTHESIS D3; SYNTHESIS quotes 0.1363 and \SI {0.1325}{V} from its own re-derivation). The two readings fit equally well, and each needs one film with an anomalous charge: \SI {6.3}{nm} with confinement and \SI {13.2}{nm} without.}}{132}{figure.caption.378}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.4}{\ignorespaces Off-state floors. (a) Measured $I_{\mathrm {D}}$ between $V_{\mathrm {G}}= -3$ and \SI {0}{V} for 2, 6.3 and \SI {13.2}{nm} at $V_{\mathrm {D}}= \SI {0.7}{V}$: flat plateaus. (b) Plateau (median over $-2$ to $-\SI {0.5}{V}$) versus thickness: $4.61\times 10^{-15}$, $1.53\times 10^{-13}$, $6.60\times 10^{-12}\ensuremath {\,\mathrm {A}/\mu \mathrm {m}}$; ratio 1432 between 13.2 and \SI {2}{nm}; log--log slope 3.78 (S4 quotes 3.85), with a $t^{3.85}$ guide. The floor rises by three orders of magnitude on an identical gate stack.}}{135}{figure.caption.390}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.5}{\ignorespaces Identifiability map. Left: parameter sets that act on the same observable of one ID--VG curve (solid arrows); right: the independent measurements that separate them (dashed arrows). Summary of \cref {tab:mech-identifiability} (SYNTHESIS Q4).}}{139}{figure.caption.397}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.1}{\ignorespaces Registered ID-VD predictions of the calibrated V1 model at 300~K, \texttt {run\_0041} (2~nm), \texttt {run\_0042} (6.3~nm, tuned) and \texttt {run\_0043} (13.2~nm), recalibrated DOS 384/192, at $V_{\mathrm {G}}= 1$, 1.5, 2, 2.5 and 3~V. Top row: full $V_{\mathrm {D}}$ range 0--3~V; bottom row: low-field zoom $V_{\mathrm {D}}$ = 0--0.3~V. All films show a strictly sub-linear low-$V_{\mathrm {D}}$ curvature (no S-shape) and a flat saturation (output conductance $<0.7$\,\% of $g_{d0}$). $I_{\mathrm {D}}(V_{\mathrm {G}}=3,V_{\mathrm {D}}=3~\mathrm {V})$ = \SI {8.687e-7}{}, \SI {7.142e-7}{} and \SI {6.388e-6}{}~\ensuremath {\,\mathrm {A}/\mu \mathrm {m}}{} (figures/MANIFEST.md).}}{144}{figure.caption.402}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.2}{\ignorespaces Predicted ratio $I_{\mathrm {D}}(T)/I_{\mathrm {D}}(300~\mathrm {K})$ versus $V_{\mathrm {G}}$ at $V_{\mathrm {D}}= 0.7$~V for 2~nm at 338.15~K (\texttt {run\_0044}) and 358.15~K (\texttt {run\_0045}), 6.3~nm at 358.15~K (\texttt {run\_0046}) and 13.2~nm at 358.15~K (\texttt {run\_0047}), relative to the 300~K references \texttt {run\_0038}, \texttt {run\_0039} ($\times 1.015074$) and \texttt {run\_0040} ($\times 1.020113$). Solid: P-phonon (as simulated, ATLAS tmu = 1.5). Dashed: P-MTR ($\times (T/300)^{1.5}$). At $V_{\mathrm {G}}= 3$~V the P-phonon/P-MTR ratios are 0.841/1.006 (2~nm, 338~K), 0.774/1.010 (2~nm, 358~K), 0.784/1.022 (6.3~nm) and 0.778/1.015 (13.2~nm); at 0.5~V the P-MTR ratio is 1.71, 2.18, 2.24 and 1.48 (figures/MANIFEST.md).}}{146}{figure.caption.409}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.3}{\ignorespaces Predicted apparent activation energy $E_{\mathrm {a}}(V_{\mathrm {G}})$ of $I_{\mathrm {D}}$ at $V_{\mathrm {D}}= 0.7$~V for both variants and the three films, least-squares Arrhenius fit over 300/338.15/358.15~K (2~nm; \texttt {run\_0038}, \texttt {run\_0044}, \texttt {run\_0045}) and 300/358.15~K (6.3~nm \texttt {run\_0039}, \texttt {run\_0046}; 13.2~nm \texttt {run\_0040}, \texttt {run\_0047}). At $V_{\mathrm {G}}= 1$~V: P-MTR 57.3/53.4/20.8~meV, P-phonon 15.1/11.1/$-21.5$~meV for 2/6.3/13.2~nm (figures/MANIFEST.md). The two variants differ by the constant tmu term $1.5\,k_{\mathrm {B}}T_{\mathrm {eff}} = 42.3$~meV.}}{147}{figure.caption.411}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.1}{\ignorespaces From atoms to device inputs: the path of this chapter. The structure is defined, the DFT calculation produces energies, bands and potentials, analysis scripts turn them into the quantities the device model uses, and these replace the literature proxies of the thickness laws.}}{159}{figure.caption.426}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.2}{\ignorespaces The self-consistent field (SCF) loop of a Kohn--Sham calculation, as implemented in Quantum ESPRESSO \citep [pp.~13--15]{Giannozzi2009}. In this study the loop stops at an estimated energy error of $10^{-9}$~Ry; it takes 25 iterations for the bulk cell and 47 for the relaxed 1~nm slab.}}{167}{figure.caption.429}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.3}{\ignorespaces Definitions used throughout the chapter (schematic, not to scale). Confinement in a thin film raises the conduction-band minimum by $\Delta E_{\mathrm {c}}$ and lowers the valence-band maximum by $\Delta E_{\mathrm {v}}$, so the gap opens by $\Delta E_{\mathrm {g}} = \Delta E_{\mathrm {c}}+ \Delta E_{\mathrm {v}}$. Measured from the vacuum level, the electron affinity (EA) and ionization potential (IP) of films of different thickness give the partition, \cref {eq:dft-partition}. The device model so far assumes $\Delta E_{\mathrm {g}} = \Delta E_{\mathrm {c}}$.}}{169}{figure.caption.434}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.4}{\ignorespaces The slab model (schematic). The calculation repeats the cell (dashed) in all directions; along $z$ the film alternates with 25~\r A{} of vacuum, which separates the periodic copies. Hydrogen atoms (open circles) terminate the surface oxygen atoms.}}{171}{figure.caption.435}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.5}{\ignorespaces Side views ($x$--$z$ projection) of the film models. (a) Relaxed 1~nm slab; (b) 2~nm slab as built from the relaxed bulk (relaxed geometry: \cref {sec:dft-calc-slab2}); (c) 1~nm IWO slab as built, with W (circled) on the central cation site of the relaxed pure slab. Dashed: the periodic cell, with vacuum above and below the film. Atoms are drawn back to front; the arrows give the distance between the outermost H atoms.}}{172}{figure.caption.436}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.6}{\ignorespaces Stages of the study and their dependencies. Solid: finished; dashed: running or queued at the time of writing. Stages 1--3 ran on the author's laptop, all others on the CERN batch system.}}{173}{figure.caption.438}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.7}{\ignorespaces Convergence of total energy, pressure and Kohn--Sham gap of bulk In$_2$O$_3${} with the plane-wave cutoff (left, 3$\times $3$\times $3) and the $k$-point grid (right, 71~Ry). The grey band marks the energy criterion of 1~mRy per atom; the dotted lines on the right mark the gap criterion of 10~meV.}}{176}{figure.caption.442}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.8}{\ignorespaces PBE band structure of bulk In$_2$O$_3${} at the relaxed lattice constant, energies relative to the valence-band maximum. Occupied bands blue, empty bands red. Path after \citet {Setyawan2010}.}}{177}{figure.caption.443}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.9}{\ignorespaces How the effective mass is obtained. Symbols: conduction-band energies from the non-SCF runs, relative to the minimum at $\Gamma $; dashed: parabola from the fit over $|k| \le 0.05$~\r A$^{-1}$ (shaded). (a) Bulk, three directions; (b) relaxed 1~nm slab, two in-plane directions. The departure from the parabola at larger $k$ is the non-parabolicity.}}{178}{figure.caption.444}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.10}{\ignorespaces Relaxation of the 1~nm slab: total energy relative to the final value, Kohn--Sham gap and total force at every SCF cycle (one per geometry). Dotted: continuation of the CPU run on a GPU from its latest geometry. The gap of the relaxed film is the one used.}}{179}{figure.caption.446}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.11}{\ignorespaces This work against the PBE calculations of \citet [pp.~21540, 21542]{Lin2022}, which used the same slab recipe: relative difference of each quantity (dots) with both values written out. Grey: $\pm 5$\,\%; green: the pre-registered tolerance of the confinement shift ($\pm 0.1$~eV; at 2~nm this is $\pm 30$\,\% and the bar is clipped). For the in-plane lattice constant of the 1~nm film, Lin et al.\ give 10.26 and 10.36~\r A; their mean is compared with ours. Below the dotted line: the 2~nm film (\cref {sec:dft-calc-slab2}); no criterion was set for the masses. The increments of Lin et al.\ are differences of their rounded values (1.27 $-$ 0.94~eV and 0.23 $-$ 0.17\,$m_0$).}}{180}{figure.caption.447}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.12}{\ignorespaces Kohn--Sham levels at $\Gamma $ near the gap, relative to the highest occupied level at $\Gamma $: bulk In$_2$O$_3${} (40-atom cell), the 1~nm film as built, and the relaxed 1~nm and 2~nm films (PBE). Blue: occupied; red: empty. Arrows: gap at $\Gamma $.}}{181}{figure.caption.448}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.13}{\ignorespaces Planar (grey) and macroscopic (blue) averages of the electrostatic potential of the relaxed 1~nm slab along the film normal, relative to the vacuum level, with the conduction- and valence-band edges (PBE). The flat regions near $z = 0$ and $z = c$ are the vacuum.}}{181}{figure.caption.449}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.14}{\ignorespaces Band edges of the films on the vacuum scale, computed from the SCF eigenvalues and the planar-averaged potential (PBE): 1~nm film as built and relaxed, relaxed 2~nm film, and 1~nm film with one W on the central 24d site. For the pure films, the band edges are taken over the whole $k$ grid, as in \cref {tab:dft-vac}. For the W-doped film, the levels at $\Gamma $ are shown: the top valence level, the bottom of the host conduction band (red; dark: filled up to $E_F$), the W-5d-derived level (purple) and $E_F$ (dashed). That column is preliminary.}}{182}{figure.caption.450}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.15}{\ignorespaces Confinement shift (left) and mass increment (right) versus film thickness: thickness laws of the device model, PBE slab values of \citet [pp.~21540, 21542]{Lin2022}, the conduction-band shift of \citet [p.~504]{Si2021} (corundum In$_2$O$_3${} on Al$_2$O$_3$, PBE), and this work (PBE, and the HSE06-corrected range of \cref {sec:dft-hse}, drawn slightly offset in thickness for visibility). Thickness of this work: H--H distance of the slab as built (0.99 and 2.02~nm). The HSE06 correction measured at 1~nm is applied unchanged at 2~nm (an assumption, not a calculation).}}{183}{figure.caption.451}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.16}{\ignorespaces Kohn--Sham gaps at $\Gamma $ of bulk In$_2$O$_3${} and of the relaxed 1~nm slab with PBE and HSE06 (norm-conserving, same geometries) and the resulting confinement shifts.}}{184}{figure.caption.453}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.17}{\ignorespaces Total density of states (grey, scaled) and W 5d projected density of states (red, scaled) relative to $E_F$: bulk 80-atom cell with W on 24d (left) and 1~nm slab with W on the central 24d site (right). PBE, preliminary.}}{186}{figure.caption.460}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.18}{\ignorespaces Data flow of the workflow. Jobs are generated on the laptop, submitted through the author's lxplus session, matched to CPU or GPU workers by HTCondor, and run by \file {driver.sh}, which takes the software from EOS and writes checkpoints there. Results return to the laptop for analysis. The \texttt {auto} loop supervises the queue.}}{190}{figure.caption.467}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.19}{\ignorespaces Left: CERN CPU usage (approximate weighted core-hours), productive and lost to faults that were fixed. Right: GPU benchmark, the same 96-atom SCF on 16 CPU cores and on one H100 NVL.}}{192}{figure.caption.472}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.20}{\ignorespaces Timeline of the CERN calculations, 27 September to 1 October 2026 (UTC). Blue: running on CPUs; red: running on a GPU; grey: queued; amber: held; hatched: failed at the first step. Dotted lines: the thread fix, the GPU validation, and the move of the hybrid check to norm-conserving pseudopotentials. Several copies of a calculation (CPU twin, GPU variants, continuations) share one row.}}{193}{figure.caption.473}%
